%META:TOPICPARENT{name="WebHome"}%
---+ Particles

%TOC%

CGV Web does not stop at calorimeter cells. The current build also draws
reconstructed tracks, topological clusters, jets, photons, electrons,
muons, taus, the missing transverse energy vector and the primary /
pile-up / secondary vertices, all from the JiveXML payload of a single
event. Each object has its own shape, colour and threshold so they can
coexist on the same canvas without becoming a soup.

This page documents every drawable object class. For cells see
[[Geometry][Geometry]]; for the threshold sliders that filter them see
[[EnergyThresholds][Energy Thresholds]].

---++ View levels

The viewer exposes three increasing levels of detail, selected from the
bottom toolbar:

   1 _Hits_ (L1): cells plus the raw track polylines.
   1 _Clusters_ (L2): everything from L1, plus the topological cluster &eta;/&phi; lines.
   1 _Particles_ (L3, default): everything from L2, plus jets, photons, electrons, muons and taus, with their floating labels and the MET arrow.

Level switching is non-destructive: every object stays in memory and is
hidden by group, not rebuilt. Moving from L3 back to L1 is instant and
loses no state.

The Particles popover (anchored to the Cluster toolbar button at L3)
gives a checkbox per class so the shifter can hide, for example, photon
springs while keeping jets and tracks visible. The button "on" indicator
reflects whether at least one class is enabled.

---++ Tracks

Reconstructed inner-detector tracks, drawn as thin solid lines, default
colour yellow =#ffea00=. The polyline samples the helix at every
inner-detector station so the line follows the field bending.

   * Per-track tooltip on hover: =p_{T}=, &eta;, &phi;, charge, number of hits, store-gate key.
   * Tracks matched to an electron, muon, jet or tau get recoloured by the match (see _Lepton matching_ below). Unmatched tracks stay yellow.
   * The =J= shortcut and the Tracks toolbar button toggle the whole group.
   * The Track tab in [[EnergyThresholds][the threshold panel]] filters by =p_{T}=.

When the cursor hovers a track, a per-hit overlay can appear (one small
sphere per inner-detector hit and per matched muon-chamber hit, sized to
stay readable at any zoom). The overlay is part of the
[[#Hits_overlay][Hits overlay]] feature.

---++ Topological clusters

Topo-clusters from the =CaloTopoCluster= algorithm. Drawn as red-orange
dashed &eta;/&phi; lines (=#ff4400=) that point from the interaction
region to the first visible calorimeter cell on the cluster axis.

   * The Cluster tab in the threshold panel filters by =E_{T}= (GeV).
   * =Shift+K= toggles the threshold itself on/off (useful when looking at sub-GeV activity around a jet).
   * The =K= shortcut shows / hides all clusters regardless of the threshold.

Clusters at L2 are the only particle class present, so the popover at L2
collapses to a simple show / hide toggle.

---++ Jets

Anti-k_t jets from the parsed jet collections. Drawn as dashed orange
&eta;/&phi; lines (=#ff8800=), wider than clusters so they read as a
distinct class.

<img src="images/particles-jets.png" alt="Event with multiple jets drawn as orange dashed eta/phi lines" style="width:100%;max-width:880px;height:auto;display:block;" />

The viewer can hold multiple jet collections per event. The active
collection is picked by the following rule, in order:

   1 =AntiKt4EMTopoJets_xAOD=, if present and non-empty (preferred default).
   1 First non-empty collection found in the XML otherwise.
   1 None if the event has no jets.

A dropdown next to the Jet tab in the threshold panel switches the
active collection without re-parsing the XML. Per-jet tooltip on hover:
=E_{T}=, =p_{T}=, energy, mass, &eta;, &phi;, store-gate key.

The Jet tab in [[EnergyThresholds][Energy Thresholds]] exposes a min
=E_{T}= filter; the slider is the only way to cull low-energy jets from
the scene. Jets carry the same calo-bound refresh logic as photons
and taus: when cell visibility changes (slider, slicer, sub-detector
toggle), the jet line endpoints are rewritten in place so they keep
landing on a visible cell.

---++ Photons

Photon candidates drawn as Feynman-style wavy springs. The spring spans
the last 40 cm before the photon hits the inner calorimeter cylinder.
Colour is yellow =#ffcc00=, distinct from the track yellow by the
helical shape.

<img src="images/particles-photons.png" alt="Photon springs reaching the inner calorimeter face" style="width:100%;max-width:880px;height:auto;display:block;" />

   * Spring radius: 20 mm, ~14 coils per metre.
   * The visible tip lands exactly on the first visible cell of the photon's path (the spring tapers off in the last 15&nbsp;% of its length so the geometry closes cleanly on the endpoint).
   * Toggle via the Particles popover or by hiding photons individually at L3.

Photons follow the calo-bound refresh: hiding a sub-detector or pushing
the energy threshold up causes the spring endpoint to migrate to
whatever cell is now first along the path.

---++ Electrons and positrons

Electron and positron candidates. The matched inner-detector track is
recoloured by the match (red =#ff3030= for e&minus;, green =#33dd55= for
e+); a small floating sprite (=e&minus;= or =e+=) sits halfway down the
track polyline inside the inner detector, away from the cell soup near
the calorimeter face.

<img src="images/particles-electrons.png" alt="Electron candidate with recoloured track and e± sprite" style="width:100%;max-width:880px;height:auto;display:block;" />

   * Charge is read from the matched track's PDG id.
   * The sprite is built once per event and never recreated by view-level toggles; only its visibility flips.
   * The match runs at L3 only. Leaving L3 reverts the track colour to the regular yellow.
   * The popover at L3 has an _Unmatched e_ toggle that hides electron candidates whose track could not be matched.

---++ Muons

Muon candidates. The matched =CombinedMuonTrack= polyline is repainted
blue =#4a90d9= by the muon-chamber-hit raycast; a small floating
=&mu;&minus;= / =&mu;+= sprite sits at the outer end of the polyline,
roughly at the muon-spectrometer edge (~9-10 m from the interaction
point), where the track is most isolated.

<img src="images/particles-muons.png" alt="Muon track repainted blue with μ sprite at the spectrometer edge" style="width:100%;max-width:880px;height:auto;display:block;" />

   * Charge is read from the track PDG id; older XMLs that drop the field render a plain =&mu;= label.
   * The sprite is part of the muon-label group; the Particles popover toggles it together with the track repaint.
   * The popover also has an _Unmatched &mu;_ toggle for muon candidates without a matching track.

Muon visibility is also coupled to the ATLAS-Ghost envelopes: enabling
the MS-side ghost reveals the muon-chamber bounding shells; the
muonAliases module keeps the sub-detector switches consistent so a hit
in the MS does not vanish when only the TileCal envelope is on.

---++ Taus

Hadronic tau candidates. Each candidate is drawn as a purple dashed
&eta;/&phi; line (=#b366ff=); for matched candidates one =&tau;&minus;=,
=&tau;+= or =&tau;= sprite is anchored on each matched daughter track at
the calo face. For three-prong taus this produces three labels that
remain readable because the daughters have separated by then.

<img src="images/particles-taus.png" alt="Hadronic tau candidate with purple dashed line and τ sprites on daughter tracks" style="width:100%;max-width:880px;height:auto;display:block;" />

   * Charge from the JiveXML =<charge>= sum. Values other than &plusmn;1 mark "unmatched" candidates that the popover can hide.
   * Tau labels respect a priority chain. If a daughter track is already claimed by a higher-priority match (electron, muon, jet), the tau label on that track is suppressed.
   * The Tau =p_{T}= threshold is in the Cluster popover at L3.

---++ Missing transverse energy

A hot pink (=#ff0066=) arrow drawn in the (x, y) plane at z = 0, pointing
in the direction of the MET vector. Length scales linearly with
magnitude (50 mm per GeV) and is clamped to stay readable across the
physics range:

   * Floor: 400 mm (visible at the lowest MET).
   * Ceiling: 6 m (does not overflow the calo envelope for high MET).
   * Arrowhead height: rescaled per frame to stay at a fixed pixel size, so direction is always readable regardless of zoom.

A small =&nu;= sprite sits past the cone, marking the direction the
missing momentum points (interpreted as the direction the undetected
neutrinos escaped).

The MET arrow is part of the Particles popover at L3.

---++ Vertices

Primary, pile-up and secondary vertices, drawn as small spheres very
close to the origin. Per-frame scaling keeps each marker at a fixed
pixel size on screen up to a world-size cap, so they stay visible at any
zoom without growing into centimetre-sized blobs.

| *Vertex* | *Colour*   | *Hex*     | *Size on screen* |
| Primary  | white      | =#ffffff= | 8 px             |
| Pile-up  | soft blue  | =#88aaff= | 4 px             |
| Secondary (b-tag) | green | =#00ff88= | 6 px         |

Vertices are stand-alone overlays: they have no threshold slider and
are not gated by the view level.

---++ Hits overlay

When the cursor enters a track line, the inner-detector and muon-chamber
hits attached to that track are revealed as small white spheres along
the polyline. Three resolution paths:

   1 Pixel and SCT hits: exact (x, y, z) from the parser.
   1 TRT hits: interpolated along the polyline at the recorded =r= (barrel) or =z= (endcap).
   1 Muon-chamber hits: kept only if within ~3 cm of the polyline; otherwise interpreted as a wire / chamber centre and dropped.

The overlay disappears as soon as the cursor leaves the track or the
canvas. Each hit stays at a constant 2 px radius on screen via per-frame
rescaling, so the dots are readable at any zoom but never overpower the
track they belong to.

The hits overlay is always-on at L1; at L2 / L3 it follows the track
hover regardless of which other classes are enabled.

---++ Lepton matching

The track-match module decides whether a reconstructed track belongs to
an electron, muon, jet or tau, using a &Delta;R distance against each
candidate. Multiple candidates can claim the same track; the priority
chain is:

   1 Electron
   1 Muon
   1 Jet (jet-tagged tracks lose their lepton label)
   1 Tau

The first match wins; lower-priority matches that lose the contest fall
back to "unmatched" and are subject to the popover toggles
(_Unmatched e_, _Unmatched &mu;_, _Unmatched &tau;_).

A track that wins an electron / muon / tau match is repainted in the
match colour (red / green / blue / purple). A track that wins a jet
match keeps the yellow track colour but is excluded from the unmatched
classes.

---++ Floating labels

Every label sprite (=e&minus;=, =e+=, =&mu;&minus;=, =&mu;+=, =&tau;=)
sits at a fixed pixel size on screen via the same per-frame rescaling as
the vertex markers. Labels never grow large when zooming in and never
disappear when zooming out.

The Particles popover at L3 also has a master _Track labels_ toggle to
hide every label at once. The toggle is independent of the per-class
visibility, so it can be used to make a clean screenshot of the
tracks-and-cells layout without hiding the lepton tracks themselves.

